---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Celebration HOA Paint Guidelines | Exterior Paint & Murals"
  description="Understanding Celebration HOA rules for paint colors, exterior painting, and interior murals. What requires approval in Celebration, FL."
  ogImage="/images/exterior-painting.jpg"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/celebration-hoa-paint-guide/#article",
        "headline": "Celebration HOA Paint Guidelines",
        "description": "Navigating Celebration HOA rules for painting and home improvements.",
        "author": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC."
        },
        "publisher": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC.",
          "logo": {
            "@type": "ImageObject",
            "url": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/images/logo.png"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/celebration-hoa-paint-guide/#breadcrumbs",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Guides",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "Celebration HOA Paint Guide",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/celebration-hoa-paint-guide"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Celebration HOA Paint Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Celebration HOA Paint Guidelines</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Understanding Celebration HOA rules for paint colors, exterior painting, and what requires approval.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, Florida has strict HOA design guidelines to maintain the community's architectural character. If you're <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting-celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">painting your home</a>, installing <a href="/services/murals-celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">murals</a>, or making exterior changes, here's what you need to know about HOA approval.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This is general educational guidance. Always check your specific HOA documents and submit Architectural Review Committee (ARC) forms before starting exterior work. HOA rules vary by neighborhood within Celebration.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Requires HOA Approval?</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Exterior Paint (Approval Required)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Any exterior paint color change requires Architectural Review Committee (ARC) approval. This includes:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>House body color</li>
            <li>Trim, shutters, doors</li>
            <li>Garage doors</li>
            <li>Fences (if permitted)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Even if you're repainting the same color, some neighborhoods require ARC notification. Check your specific village guidelines. See our general <a href="/guides/hoa-and-exterior-paint-osceola" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">HOA paint guide</a> for Osceola County.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Interior Paint (No Approval Needed)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            You can paint interior walls any color without HOA approval. Interior murals, wallpaper, and decorating are entirely up to you. HOA rules apply only to what's visible from the street or common areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Exterior Murals (Rarely Allowed)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Exterior murals visible from the street are typically not approved in Celebration. The community maintains traditional architectural aesthetics. Interior murals in kids rooms or home theaters don't require approval.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Approved Exterior Paint Colors</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Celebration HOA provides approved color palettes that fit the community's design standards. Colors vary by architectural style and village:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">General Guidelines</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Neutral tones:</strong> Whites, creams, soft grays, beiges, tans</li>
            <li><strong>Historic pastels:</strong> Pale yellow, soft blue, sage green (style-dependent)</li>
            <li><strong>Earth tones:</strong> Terracotta, warm browns (Mediterranean or Spanish styles)</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid:</strong> Bright colors, neon, dark colors (navy, black, deep red)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Each architectural style (Coastal, Colonial, Mediterranean, Victorian, etc.) has specific approved palettes. Request the color chart from your ARC before selecting paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Trim & Accent Colors</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Trim, shutters, and doors typically use white, cream, or colors from the approved palette. Bold accent colors (bright red door, turquoise shutters) usually aren't approved unless they match your home's original approved design.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">HOA Approval Process</h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 1: Check Your HOA Documents</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Review your Celebration Community Standards or village-specific guidelines. These outline paint rules, approved colors, and submission requirements. Documents are usually available on the Celebration HOA website or through your property management company.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 2: Submit ARC Application</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Complete an Architectural Review Committee (ARC) application. Include:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Paint color samples or brand/color codes (e.g., Sherwin-Williams "Accessible Beige")</li>
            <li>Photos of your home showing areas to be painted</li>
            <li>Scope of work (full repaint vs. trim only)</li>
            <li>Contractor information (if applicable)</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 3: Wait for Approval</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            ARC reviews typically take 2-4 weeks. Don't start painting until you receive written approval. Unapproved work may require repainting at your expense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 4: Complete Work & Notify HOA</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Once approved, complete painting within the timeframe specified (usually 60-90 days). Some HOAs require notification when work is complete.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common HOA Violations to Avoid</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Painting without approval:</strong> Biggest mistake. Submit ARC request first.</li>
            <li><strong>Unauthorized colors:</strong> Even if you love it, unapproved colors must be repainted.</li>
            <li><strong>Peeling or faded paint:</strong> HOAs require homes be well-maintained. Peeling paint can result in violation notices.</li>
            <li><strong>Mismatched touch-ups:</strong> Spot-painting that doesn't match draws attention. Either match perfectly or repaint the full section.</li>
            <li><strong>Incomplete projects:</strong> Leaving primer visible or partially painted trim for months violates maintenance standards.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Interior Painting & Murals (No HOA Approval)</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Interior work doesn't require HOA approval. You're free to:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Paint walls any color</li>
            <li>Install <a href="/services/murals-celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">custom murals</a> in kids rooms, nurseries, or home theaters</li>
            <li>Add <a href="/services/wallpaper-celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">wallpaper</a> or accent walls</li>
            <li>Paint cabinets, trim, or ceilings</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            HOA rules apply only to exteriors and items visible from common areas. Your interior is your creative space.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Working with Contractors in Celebration</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Hire contractors familiar with Celebration HOA rules. We've completed dozens of Celebration paint projects and know the approval process. We can:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Recommend approved colors from your palette</li>
            <li>Provide color samples and documentation for ARC submission</li>
            <li>Complete work to Celebration's quality standards</li>
            <li>Work within approved project timelines</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Best Practices for Celebration Homeowners</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Plan ahead:</strong> Submit ARC requests 4-6 weeks before you want to start painting</li>
            <li><strong>Choose from approved palettes:</strong> Makes approval faster and avoids rejection</li>
            <li><strong>Keep records:</strong> Save ARC approval letters in case of future questions or resale</li>
            <li><strong>Match original colors:</strong> Repainting in original approved colors usually speeds approval</li>
            <li><strong>Maintain regularly:</strong> Touch up peeling paint promptly to avoid violation notices</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Resources</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Celebration HOA website:</strong> Download ARC forms and color palettes</li>
            <li><strong>Property management company:</strong> Ask questions about your specific neighborhood rules</li>
            <li><strong>Architectural Review Committee:</strong> Email or call with color questions before submitting</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="text-center mt-12">
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-6 font-sans">Need help with painting in Celebration?</p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Celebration Painting & Home Improvements"
    description="HOA-compliant painting and repairs. Serving Celebration, FL homeowners."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
